<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <p>
    In what follows, we illustrate good indicators that can help you in thinking on how to
    incorporate the six dimensions in each context: Curriculum (C), Pedagogical Practices (P),
    and Structure of the program (S).
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Interpersonal relationships and well-being</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Include icebreaker activities and places for students to share about themselves.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Teach mentors to share about themselves and remember the info the students share.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: Mentors are enthusiastic and display a positive attitude toward working with students.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Students at the center</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Activities are designed to promote collaboration and give students the opportunity to make choices.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Allow students to explore activities and help each other rather than being led by mentors.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: Mentors stand and walk around the student tables to make sure they are available to help every student.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Deep critical &amp; logical thinking</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Activities are built to challenge students using concepts that are new to them.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Mentors are taught to scaffold activities according to student interest and knowledge. Students are not expected to complete every activity, but instead asked to spend time at whatever part of the activity challenges them.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: Program is run on a college campus with undergraduate students serving as mentors to give students a sense of what college is like.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Relevance, Friendly Language and Representations</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Activities are designed to be solved in multiple ways and clearly explained with references to real world settings the students are familiar with.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Mentors are trained on multiple methods of solving problems and how to intuitively explain the concepts behind each activity at the level of the students.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: Students are provided with tools and manipulatives that encourage them to explore in multiple ways.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Equity through embracing cultural diversity</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Activities are created with cultural connections in mind.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Mentors are informed about cultural connections of activities and encouraged to talk with students and celebrate their culture.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: Program elements like certain snacks and decorations enhance cultural connections.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Promoting Social Justice</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>C: Activities explore ideas of fairness, justice, and equality.</p></li>
      <li><p>P: Mentors are taught to emphasize the importance of social justice and ask students about how they feel about inequality and make connections to real life.</p></li>
      <li><p>S: The program targets working with under-served students.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
